ren":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a "},{"type":"link","href":"./queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"queuer"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It preserves pending operations and processes them according to FIFO, LIFO, or priority ordering. A finite "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" can still cause new items to be rejected."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"several-items-should-run-together","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Several items should run together"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a "},{"type":"link","href":"./batching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"batcher"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It collects items until a size, time, or custom condition triggers one batch execution."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"synchronous-or-asynchronous","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Synchronous or asynchronous"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each utility has a synchronous and asynchronous version. Start with the synchronous version unless the utility must manage Promise-specific behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the asynchronous version when you need to:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Await the wrapped function's result."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Track success, error, and settlement state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure error propagation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retry failed executions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Abort in-flight operations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run queued tasks concurrently with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncQueuer"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Passing an async function to a synchronous utility does not provide these features. The synchronous utility invokes the function but does not await or manage its Promise."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Synchronous"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Asynchronous"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./debouncing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debouncing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./async-debouncing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async debouncing"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./throttling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Throttling"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./async-throttling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async throttling"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./rate-limiting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate limiting"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./async-rate-limiting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async rate limiting"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queuing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./async-queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async queuing"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./batching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Batching"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./async-batching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async batching"}]}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The async utilities use "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-retrying.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncRetryer"}]},{"type":"text","value":" internally for retry and abort support."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"core-package-or-framework-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core package or framework adapter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/pacer"},{"type":"text","value":" when you need the core classes and functions without component lifecycle integration."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a framework adapter in an application that needs automatic cleanup and reactive state:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../framework/react/adapter.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React adapter"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../framework/preact/adapter.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preact adapter"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../framework/solid/adapter.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid adapter"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../framework/angular/adapter.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Angular adapter"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Framework adapters provide several API shapes around the same underlying utility:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Instance APIs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useDebouncer"},{"type":"text","value":" expose lifecycle methods and selected state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callback APIs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useDebouncedCallback"},{"type":"text","value":" return a function to call."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State and value APIs connect the utility to framework state."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the narrowest API that provides the control you need. Use the instance API when you need methods such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pacer-lite-or-pacer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pacer Lite or Pacer"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/pacer-lite"},{"type":"text","value":" is intended for libraries that need smaller, non-reactive utilities. It omits TanStack Store integration, framework adapters, Devtools support, and some advanced options."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the regular core package or a framework adapter for application code. Consider Pacer Lite when bundle size is the primary constraint and reactive state is unnecessary."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"compare-the-utilities","text":"Compare the utilities","level":2},{"id":"choose-by-required-behavior","text":"Choose by required behavior","level":2},{"id":"only-the-final-value-matters","text":"Only the final value matters","level":3},{"id":"work-should-continue-at-a-steady-pace","text":"Work should continue at a steady pace","level":3},{"id":"a-fixed-quota-must-be-enforced","text":"A fixed quota must be enforced","level":3},{"id":"every-operation-must-run","text":"Every operation must run","level":3},{"id":"several-items-should-run-together","text":"Several items should run together","level":3},{"id":"synchronous-or-asynchronous","text":"Synchronous or asynchronous","level":2},{"id":"core-package-or-framework-adapter","text":"Core package or framework adapter","level":2},{"id":"pacer-lite-or-pacer","text":"Pacer Lite or Pacer","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Pacer provides five strategies for controlling when operations run. The right choice depends on what should happen to calls that arrive faster than your application can process them.\n\n## Compare the utilities\n\n| Utility | What happens to frequent calls? | Best fit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [Debouncer](./debouncing.md) | Earlier calls are discarded. The latest call runs after activity stops. | Search input, validation, autosave |\n| [Throttler](./throttling.md) | Calls are limited to a steady interval. A trailing call can retain the latest arguments. | Scroll, resize, progress, repeated UI updates |\n| [Rate Limiter](./rate-limiting.md) | Calls run until a quota is reached. Additional calls are rejected until capacity returns. | Client-side quotas and burst limits |\n| [Queuer](./queuing.md) | Calls wait in an ordered buffer and run individually. | Work that must not be lost |\n| [Batcher](./batching.md) | Items accumulate and run together as one batch. | Bulk requests, writes, and analytics events |\n\n## Choose by required behavior\n\n### Only the final value matters\n\nUse a [debouncer](./debouncing.md). Every call restarts a timer, and the most recent call runs once the calls go quiet.\n\n### Work should continue at a steady pace\n\nUse a [throttler](./throttling.md). It limits execution frequency without waiting for activity to stop completely.\n\n### A fixed quota must be enforced\n\nUse a [rate limiter](./rate-limiting.md). It accepts calls until the configured limit is reached, then rejects additional calls within the window.\n\n### Every operation must run\n\nUse a [queuer](./queuing.md). It preserves pending operations and processes them according to FIFO, LIFO, or priority ordering. A finite `maxSize` can still cause new items to be rejected.\n\n### Several items should run together\n\nUse a [batcher](./batching.md). It collects items until a size, time, or custom condition triggers one batch execution.\n\n## Synchronous or asynchronous\n\nEach utility has a synchronous and asynchronous version. Start with the synchronous version unless the utility must manage Promise-specific behavior.\n\nUse the asynchronous version when you need to:\n\n- Await the wrapped function's result.\n- Track success, error, and settlement state.\n- Configure error propagation.\n- Retry failed executions.\n- Abort in-flight operations.\n- Run queued tasks concurrently with `AsyncQueuer`.\n\nPassing an async function to a synchronous utility does not provide these features. The synchronous utility invokes the function but does not await or manage its Promise.\n\n| Synchronous | Asynchronous |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Debouncing](./debouncing.md) | [Async debouncing](./async-debouncing.md) |\n| [Throttling](./throttling.md) | [Async throttling](./async-throttling.md) |\n| [Rate limiting](./rate-limiting.md) | [Async rate limiting](./async-rate-limiting.md) |\n| [Queuing](./queuing.md) | [Async queuing](./async-queuing.md) |\n| [Batching](./batching.md) | [Async batching](./async-batching.md) |\n\nThe async utilities use [`AsyncRetryer`](./async-retrying.md) internally for retry and abort support.\n\n## Core package or framework adapter\n\nUse `@tanstack/pacer` when you need the core classes and functions without component lifecycle integration.\n\nUse a framework adapter in an application that needs automatic cleanup and reactive state:\n\n- [React adapter](../framework/react/adapter.md)\n- [Preact adapter](../framework/preact/adapter.md)\n- [Solid adapter](../framework/solid/adapter.md)\n- [Angular adapter](../framework/angular/adapter.md)\n\nFramework adapters provide several API shapes around the same underlying utility:\n\n- Instance APIs such as `useDebouncer` expose lifecycle methods and selected state.\n- Callback APIs such as `useDebouncedCallback` return a function to call.\n- State and value APIs connect the utility to framework state.\n\nChoose the narrowest API that provides the control you need. Use the instance API when you need methods such as `cancel()` or `flush()`.\n\n## Pacer Lite or Pacer\n\n`@tanstack/pacer-lite` is intended for libraries that need smaller, non-reactive utilities. It omits TanStack Store integration, framework adapters, Devtools support, and some advanced options.\n\nUse the regular core package or a framework adapter for application code. Consider Pacer Lite when bundle size is the primary constraint and reactive state is unnecessary.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Which Pacer Utility Should I Choose?\nid: which-pacer-utility-should-i-choose\n---\n\nTanStack Pacer provides five strategies for controlling when operations run. The right choice depends on what should happen to calls that arrive faster than your application can process them.\n\n## Compare the utilities\n\n| Utility | What happens to frequent calls? | Best fit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [Debouncer](./debouncing.md) | Earlier calls are discarded. The latest call runs after activity stops. | Search input, validation, autosave |\n| [Throttler](./throttling.md) | Calls are limited to a steady interval. A trailing call can retain the latest arguments. | Scroll, resize, progress, repeated UI updates |\n| [Rate Limiter](./rate-limiting.md) | Calls run until a quota is reached. Additional calls are rejected until capacity returns. | Client-side quotas and burst limits |\n| [Queuer](./queuing.md) | Calls wait in an ordered buffer and run individually. | Work that must not be lost |\n| [Batcher](./batching.md) | Items accumulate and run together as one batch. | Bulk requests, writes, and analytics events |\n\n## Choose by required behavior\n\n### Only the final value matters\n\nUse a [debouncer](./debouncing.md). Every call restarts a timer, and the most recent call runs once the calls go quiet.\n\n### Work should continue at a steady pace\n\nUse a [throttler](./throttling.md). It limits execution frequency without waiting for activity to stop completely.\n\n### A fixed quota must be enforced\n\nUse a [rate limiter](./rate-limiting.md). It accepts calls until the configured limit is reached, then rejects additional calls within the window.\n\n### Every operation must run\n\nUse a [queuer](./queuing.md). It preserves pending operations and processes them according to FIFO, LIFO, or priority ordering. A finite `maxSize` can still cause new items to be rejected.\n\n### Several items should run together\n\nUse a [batcher](./batching.md). It collects items until a size, time, or custom condition triggers one batch execution.\n\n## Synchronous or asynchronous\n\nEach utility has a synchronous and asynchronous version. Start with the synchronous version unless the utility must manage Promise-specific behavior.\n\nUse the asynchronous version when you need to:\n\n- Await the wrapped function's result.\n- Track success, error, and settlement state.\n- Configure error propagation.\n- Retry failed executions.\n- Abort in-flight operations.\n- Run queued tasks concurrently with `AsyncQueuer`.\n\nPassing an async function to a synchronous utility does not provide these features. The synchronous utility invokes the function but does not await or manage its Promise.\n\n| Synchronous | Asynchronous |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Debouncing](./debouncing.md) | [Async debouncing](./async-debouncing.md) |\n| [Throttling](./throttling.md) | [Async throttling](./async-throttling.md) |\n| [Rate limiting](./rate-limiting.md) | [Async rate limiting](./async-rate-limiting.md) |\n| [Queuing](./queuing.md) | [Async queuing](./async-queuing.md) |\n| [Batching](./batching.md) | [Async batching](./async-batching.md) |\n\nThe async utilities use [`AsyncRetryer`](./async-retrying.md) internally for retry and abort support.\n\n## Core package or framework adapter\n\nUse `@tanstack/pacer` when you need the core classes and functions without component lifecycle integration.\n\nUse a framework adapter in an application that needs automatic cleanup and reactive state:\n\n- [React adapter](../framework/react/adapter.md)\n- [Preact adapter](../framework/preact/adapter.md)\n- [Solid adapter](../framework/solid/adapter.md)\n- [Angular adapter](../framework/angular/adapter.md)\n\nFramework adapters provide several API shapes around the same underlying utility:\n\n- Instance APIs such as `useDebouncer` expose lifecycle methods and selected state.\n- Callback APIs such as `useDebouncedCallback` return a function to call.\n- State and value APIs connect the utility to framework state.\n\nChoose the narrowest API that provides the control you need. Use the instance API when you need methods such as `cancel()` or `flush()`.\n\n## Pacer Lite or Pacer\n\n`@tanstack/pacer-lite` is intended for libraries that need smaller, non-reactive utilities. It omits TanStack Store integration, framework adapters, Devtools support, and some advanced options.\n\nUse the regular core package or a framework adapter for application code. Consider Pacer Lite when bundle size is the primary constraint and reactive state is unnecessary.\n"}
